  "id": "b1640c4d-ce76-50bc-b6d0-b8e03b68d402",
  "slug": "festive-fruitcake-i",
  "name": "Festive Fruitcake I",
  "recipe_type": "dessert",
  "brief_description": "This is a large recipe that is rich and dark. Make a few weeks ahead of time. Apple juice can be substituted for orange juice. Originally submitted to CakeRecipe.com.",
  "cuisine": null,
  "instructions": [
    {
      "position": 1,
      "text": "Preheat oven to 275 degrees F (135 degrees C). Grease four 9 x 5 x 3 inch loaf pans, and then line with brown paper or foil. Grease again."
    },
    {
      "position": 2,
      "text": "In a large container stir raisins, currants, candied fruit mix, candied cherries, maraschino cherries, dates, and 1 cup flour together until all the fruit is well coated with flour."
    },
    {
      "position": 3,
      "text": "Measure 2 1/4 cup flour, baking powder, baking soda, salt, cocoa, cinnamon, and nutmeg into a medium bowl. Stir to mix."
    },
    {
      "position": 4,
      "text": "In a very large bowl, cream the butter or margarine with the sugar. Beat in egg yolks three at a time. Stir in pineapple with juice and vanilla. Add flour mixture in 3 parts alternately with fruit juice in 2 parts to the creamed mixture, beginning and ending with flour mixture. Stir in walnuts."
    },
    {
      "position": 5,
      "text": "In another bowl, beat egg whites until stiff. Fold egg whites into batter, and stir in fruit gently. Divide batter among pans. Smooth."
    },
    {
      "position": 6,
      "text": "Bake for about 3 hours until an inserted wooden pick comes out clean."
    }
  ],
  "servings": 48,
